WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
on the data protection topic.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.880
Thank you. Good morning, everyone.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.550
It is a pleasure to be with all of you here today.

4
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:15.080
My name is Yoana Koleva and I'm part of GATE Institute, where I'm

5
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:20.710
responsible for data and AI management and their protection.

6
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.200
So today I'm going to talk to you about how data governance and data

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:33.630
management are the foundation for data security and cyber defense.

8
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.590
But let's start with a few

9
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.350
words about who we are first.

10
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.590
GATE was founded in 2019

11
00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.640
as the first center of excellence for big data and AI in Eastern

12
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.520
Europe, in a strategic collaboration with

13
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:58.150
Sofia University and Chalmers University in Sweden.

14
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.030
Together with government, industry and startups, we work

15
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:12.000
on big data and AI research, innovation and education within three

16
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.800
key domains, and these are future cities, digital health and

17
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.750
information environment analysis.

18
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.240
We are the Bulgarian hub for the International Data Spaces

19
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.760
Association, which works on secure and trusted data sharing between

20
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.000
organizations. We are also an accredited test center within NATO

21
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.710
DIANA, where we test and validate dual-use technologies

22
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.110
that serve both civilian and defense needs.

23
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.350
So you can see that we work with quite a lot of

24
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.800
sensitive data, from city sensor data through health data to

25
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.870
technologies with defense applications.

26
00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:05.332
And what we have learned is that usually the data that is most

27
00:02:05.332 --> 00:02:08.082
useful to us is also the most sensitive.

28
00:02:08.572 --> 00:02:15.172
Therefore, we treat data governance and data security as one task.

29
00:02:15.172 --> 00:02:18.122
And I'm going to show you how we do that in more detail,

30
00:02:18.652 --> 00:02:21.322
but let's look into a few numbers first.

31
00:02:21.892 --> 00:02:27.612
In 53% of the breaches, customers' personal data has been

32
00:02:27.612 --> 00:02:32.652
exposed. And this is the type of data that organizations lose most

33
00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:38.412
often. The second figure shows us that when the data is scattered

34
00:02:38.412 --> 00:02:42.772
across different environments, for example, on premises,

35
00:02:42.772 --> 00:02:47.972
in the cloud, in different systems, a breach costs on average about

36
00:02:47.972 --> 00:02:50.042
$5 million. The third figure says that it takes approximately

37
00:02:53.772 --> 00:02:59.482
241 days, or 8 months, to find and contain a breach.

38
00:03:00.372 --> 00:03:05.492
Also, about 40% of employees say that they share companies'

39
00:03:05.492 --> 00:03:11.202
sensitive data with AI tools that are not approved by their employer.

40
00:03:11.692 --> 00:03:14.692
And this is what people admit in a survey.

41
00:03:14.692 --> 00:03:17.402
In reality, that number is probably a lot higher.

42
00:03:18.212 --> 00:03:22.212
So, you can see that none of these numbers is about failures of

43
00:03:22.212 --> 00:03:27.332
technologies. They're failures of knowing what data we have,

44
00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:30.642
who owns the data, and who has access to it.

45
00:03:31.492 --> 00:03:36.482
In most companies, data grows faster than anyone can keep up.

46
00:03:37.572 --> 00:03:42.122
We still store data in old systems.

47
00:03:42.572 --> 00:03:46.722
We don't assign owners, or we don't classify it as sensitive.

48
00:03:47.612 --> 00:03:51.492
These are patterns that, on their own, look like everyday

49
00:03:51.492 --> 00:03:56.532
inconveniences, but I'm going to show you a few examples of what

50
00:03:56.532 --> 00:03:59.962
happens when these patterns meet an attacker.

51
00:04:00.532 --> 00:04:04.654
So, we are going to look into three simple scenarios that are based

52
00:04:04.654 --> 00:04:08.244
on those patterns and that result in incidents.

53
00:04:09.374 --> 00:04:12.004
The first one is an online retailer.

54
00:04:12.734 --> 00:04:17.774
Now, I'm going to use an example, Ivan Petrov,

55
00:04:17.774 --> 00:04:19.884
who is one of their best customers.

56
00:04:20.454 --> 00:04:25.014
And as you can see, his data lives in four different

57
00:04:25.014 --> 00:04:28.844
systems. And in each system, there is different data.

58
00:04:29.254 --> 00:04:33.204
So, for example, the marketing knows him with his personal email,

59
00:04:33.654 --> 00:04:38.204
the sales team with his mobile number, the product team knows his

60
00:04:39.414 --> 00:04:43.804
user credentials, and finance knows him with his company's name.

61
00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:47.964
Now, none of these systems is connected to one another.

62
00:04:48.614 --> 00:04:52.374
And also you can see that the data is not consistent,

63
00:04:52.374 --> 00:04:54.084
and that will be a challenging task.

64
00:04:54.974 --> 00:05:00.214
But what happens is an attacker sends a phishing email to someone

65
00:05:00.214 --> 00:05:04.734
in the marketing team. And they manage to get hold of Ivan's email

66
00:05:04.734 --> 00:05:07.164
address. And then they send him an email.

67
00:05:07.894 --> 00:05:12.574
Then, in that email, there is a fake login page where he enters

68
00:05:12.574 --> 00:05:17.134
his password. And from there, they can go to the product and see

69
00:05:17.134 --> 00:05:21.854
all his invoices. Now, after that, they will reach out to the

70
00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:27.604
finance team as Ivan and ask for a refund of one of his invoices

71
00:05:28.654 --> 00:05:30.604
to a new bank account.

72
00:05:31.014 --> 00:05:35.294
Now, because the data is so

73
00:05:35.294 --> 00:05:40.044
fragmented, the finance team has no way of cross-referencing

74
00:05:40.694 --> 00:05:42.684
that against the other systems.

75
00:05:43.254 --> 00:05:46.974
So, what they can see is the invoice number is the same,

76
00:05:46.974 --> 00:05:50.814
the name matches, the amount is also correct.

77
00:05:50.814 --> 00:05:55.534
So, there is a likelihood that they're going to send the refund

78
00:05:55.534 --> 00:05:58.814
to the attacker. Also, when the breach happens,

79
00:05:58.814 --> 00:06:02.124
the regulator will ask how many customers have been affected.

80
00:06:02.694 --> 00:06:06.575
Now, there is no way for them to know that, because only for Ivan you

81
00:06:06.575 --> 00:06:11.015
can see he exists as four different customers across the

82
00:06:11.015 --> 00:06:15.245
systems. The second example is an example of a municipality.

83
00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:20.375
Now, there is a project and a vendor has been given a temporary

84
00:06:20.375 --> 00:06:25.245
access through a shared account to the citizens' complaints database.

85
00:06:26.215 --> 00:06:30.135
Now, in that database there is a lot of personal data,

86
00:06:30.135 --> 00:06:35.135
the citizens' names, their addresses, who they complained about,

87
00:06:35.135 --> 00:06:37.805
personal ID numbers, and so on.

88
00:06:38.255 --> 00:06:39.525
So, after the project

89
00:06:41.215 --> 00:06:46.535
ends, nobody closed the access of the vendor to the

90
00:06:46.535 --> 00:06:52.175
account. After a few months, the vendor was

91
00:06:52.175 --> 00:06:57.175
breached. So, now the attacker got hold of the account that was

92
00:06:57.175 --> 00:06:59.965
forgotten and now they have access to the database.

93
00:07:01.695 --> 00:07:07.015
So, you can see, with the amount of personal

94
00:07:07.015 --> 00:07:10.855
data that there is in the database, you can only imagine the scale

95
00:07:10.855 --> 00:07:13.085
of the breach and the fraud that can happen.

96
00:07:13.615 --> 00:07:19.215
And also the city has no way of figuring out what was

97
00:07:19.215 --> 00:07:21.485
affected because the account was shared.

98
00:07:22.335 --> 00:07:25.725
And the third example is an example of an AI.

99
00:07:26.255 --> 00:07:29.605
So, a company is rolling out an AI assistant across

100
00:07:30.575 --> 00:07:34.135
all their employees that is connected to the email addresses,

101
00:07:34.135 --> 00:07:37.365
to the shared drives, to all the company's data.

102
00:07:38.495 --> 00:07:41.575
Now, imagine an employee is asking the question,

103
00:07:41.575 --> 00:07:44.655
what are the salaries of the management team?

104
00:07:44.655 --> 00:07:46.815
And the assistant is going to answer.

105
00:07:46.815 --> 00:07:52.775
Why? Because there is a payroll file that is lying around in

106
00:07:52.775 --> 00:07:56.695
one of the shared folders that is shared with the whole company,

107
00:07:56.695 --> 00:08:00.205
but nobody has classified that data as confidential.

108
00:08:01.135 --> 00:08:08.257
Now, also imagine an attacker with a stolen password who manages to

109
00:08:08.257 --> 00:08:10.577
get hold of that assistant?

110
00:08:10.577 --> 00:08:15.217
Now, what used to take them days to find information is now

111
00:08:15.217 --> 00:08:19.897
within a couple of questions if the company has deployed AI.

112
00:08:19.897 --> 00:08:23.897
So, you can see three different examples, three different types of

113
00:08:23.897 --> 00:08:27.407
attacks, but the overarching theme is data management.

114
00:08:28.097 --> 00:08:32.737
How we manage our data can determine how much damage we're going to

115
00:08:32.737 --> 00:08:36.247
go through. Most organizations

116
00:08:37.337 --> 00:08:42.897
implement their security at the end of the data process,

117
00:08:42.897 --> 00:08:47.497
when we add monitoring, alerts, firewalls, and watch if something

118
00:08:47.497 --> 00:08:52.417
wrong happens. Well, at that point, the risk has already been

119
00:08:52.417 --> 00:08:57.937
created. And that is why data governance should always be the first

120
00:08:57.937 --> 00:09:01.737
step towards that. So, how do we start the data governance?

121
00:09:01.737 --> 00:09:04.607
Here are a few simple steps from where to start.

122
00:09:05.057 --> 00:09:08.287
Firstly, it's data inventory.

123
00:09:08.817 --> 00:09:13.617
Now, this is not the most glamorous task, but it is your baseline.

124
00:09:13.617 --> 00:09:14.687
It is the foundation.

125
00:09:15.737 --> 00:09:17.727
Firstly, what data do we have?

126
00:09:18.137 --> 00:09:20.047
Where is the data stored?

127
00:09:20.777 --> 00:09:25.767
Who actually uses the data as opposed to who has access to it?

128
00:09:26.297 --> 00:09:28.647
Once we have the answers to those questions,

129
00:09:29.137 --> 00:09:34.697
we have to evaluate all our data sets against how much value and how

130
00:09:34.697 --> 00:09:37.207
much risk they bring to the business.

131
00:09:37.617 --> 00:09:39.287
And depending on where

132
00:09:41.137 --> 00:09:46.697
in the assessment scale these data sets land,

133
00:09:46.697 --> 00:09:49.847
you can map them against the matrix you see on the right.

134
00:09:50.377 --> 00:09:57.177
So, for example, the top right corner says that the data is

135
00:09:57.177 --> 00:09:58.937
high-value, high-risk data.

136
00:09:58.937 --> 00:10:02.137
This is the most critical data of the business.

137
00:10:02.137 --> 00:10:06.337
These are the customer transactions, the payment data,

138
00:10:06.337 --> 00:10:10.498
contracts. So, most of your security efforts should be focused

139
00:10:10.498 --> 00:10:16.538
there. Then we have the top left quadrant, which is high-value low

140
00:10:16.538 --> 00:10:22.578
risk data. Now, these are the product catalogs,

141
00:10:22.578 --> 00:10:24.888
prices, internal reports.

142
00:10:25.498 --> 00:10:28.858
This is important information, but it's not a secret.

143
00:10:28.858 --> 00:10:33.138
So, the risk here is not that the data is going to be stolen,

144
00:10:33.138 --> 00:10:35.328
it is that it's going to be changed incorrectly.

145
00:10:36.018 --> 00:10:41.698
So, therefore, the focus here should be on data quality checks and

146
00:10:41.698 --> 00:10:44.768
controls over who can change the data.

147
00:10:45.378 --> 00:10:48.738
Then we have the low-risk low-value data.

148
00:10:48.738 --> 00:10:52.938
These are your old logs, forgotten CSV files and exports,

149
00:10:52.938 --> 00:10:59.528
test data, etc. This data on its own is harmless,

150
00:11:00.138 --> 00:11:04.538
but it piles up and companies pay each month for that data to be

151
00:11:04.538 --> 00:11:09.418
stored. Also, if we start really digging into that data,

152
00:11:09.418 --> 00:11:12.768
this data very often holds surprises.

153
00:11:13.818 --> 00:11:19.418
And for example, very often if you look into your testing

154
00:11:19.418 --> 00:11:23.698
data, you see that there are high chances of actual customer

155
00:11:23.698 --> 00:11:28.138
data being there. So, here the recommendation is set retention rules

156
00:11:28.138 --> 00:11:31.058
and delete the data when the time is up.

157
00:11:31.058 --> 00:11:35.738
Lastly, we have the low-value but high-risk data.

158
00:11:35.738 --> 00:11:37.928
Now, this is the one I would like you to remember.

159
00:11:38.698 --> 00:11:45.338
These are your old HR records, customer data that sits

160
00:11:45.338 --> 00:11:51.168
in old systems. These are files from projects that ended ages ago.

161
00:11:51.858 --> 00:11:55.648
Now, this data brings to the business almost nothing,

162
00:11:56.258 --> 00:12:00.698
but very often it is stored in legacy systems that are poorly

163
00:12:00.698 --> 00:12:04.648
protected. Now, this is the easiest target for the attackers.

164
00:12:05.538 --> 00:12:10.698
And if the data leaks, that comes with all the fines,

165
00:12:10.698 --> 00:12:15.139
notifications, and regulatory and reputational damage that come with

166
00:12:15.139 --> 00:12:20.379
it. So, here the advice is delete what you legally can and encrypt

167
00:12:20.379 --> 00:12:22.289
the data that you must keep.

168
00:12:23.179 --> 00:12:27.819
Now, because I'm conscious of time, if there are just three

169
00:12:27.819 --> 00:12:30.579
things to remember from this presentation today,

170
00:12:30.579 --> 00:12:35.619
let it be these. Firstly, know your data, do the inventory of the

171
00:12:35.619 --> 00:12:41.899
data. Go through the matrix of how valuable and how risky the

172
00:12:41.899 --> 00:12:45.139
data is. Then, it's the human element.

173
00:12:45.139 --> 00:12:48.819
Your employees are working and manipulating the data.

174
00:12:48.819 --> 00:12:53.099
So, give them clear instructions on how to use the data and which

175
00:12:53.099 --> 00:12:54.659
tools they're allowed to use.

176
00:12:54.659 --> 00:12:58.259
And also, for example, what type of data they're not allowed

177
00:12:58.259 --> 00:13:03.859
to put in AI. And lastly, be prepared when things go wrong because

178
00:13:03.859 --> 00:13:06.819
most probably things will go wrong.

179
00:13:06.819 --> 00:13:10.859
And at that point, if your data governance is intact,

180
00:13:10.859 --> 00:13:14.459
if your critical data is properly locked, then you'll be in a

181
00:13:14.459 --> 00:13:18.659
position to answer all the questions that come up your way and come

182
00:13:18.659 --> 00:13:21.459
up with the appropriate next actions.

183
00:13:21.459 --> 00:13:22.489
Thank you very much.
